const intro={"n":18,"title":"After Old Vizcacha's Death: Fear, Burial and Abandonment","nums":[817,823,825],"summary":"The younger son stays alone with the dead Vizcacha and his dogs while others arrange the burial. Although his guardian mistreated him, the boy places a scapular on the body and prays for him. Frightened by the animals' howling and stories about supernatural signs, he abandons the hut. He later hears that Vizcacha was buried without a proper wake and that a dog damaged the exposed corpse. The boy continues to suffer from memories and nightmares.","people":"Martín Fierro's younger son narrating; the deceased Viejo Vizcacha; a worker who later buries him; neighbors whose beliefs and reports the boy recalls.","themes":"Orphanhood and isolation; compassion despite abuse; burial and mourning; superstition; reported accounts versus firsthand knowledge.","words":"escapulario (devotional scapular); rosario (rosary); güerfano (orphan); finado (deceased person); guasquerío (leather straps); peón (hired rural worker).","questions":["Why does the younger son pray for a guardian who treated him poorly?","Which events does he witness and which are reported to him afterward?","What role do the dogs' howling and the neighbors' beliefs play in the frightening scene?","How do the boy's recurring dreams show the aftermath of his experience?"],"analysis":"The boy witnesses the lonely vigil but learns the circumstances of the burial later from someone else. The neighbors' beliefs about the dogs are presented as folk superstition, not established supernatural events. His compassion for Vizcacha does not negate the guardian's previous neglect.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-17/","relatedTitle":"Vizcacha’s death and estate inventory"};
